#!/usr/bin/env php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

// **JIT on, because this repository is a geometry solver wearing a console application.**
//
// **THIS LINE ALONE CANNOT TURN IT ON, AND ON 2026-09-11 IT WAS MEASURABLY NOT ON.** The comment that used to
// stand here claimed the image ships a 64 MB JIT buffer with only `opcache.jit` left empty. Measured in the
// project container: `opcache.jit_buffer_size => 0` and `opcache_get_status()['jit']['enabled'] === false`.
// `opcache.jit_buffer_size` is `PHP_INI_SYSTEM`, it defaults to 0 and `ini_set()` cannot raise it, so with no
// buffer this call selects a mode for a compiler with nowhere to emit code.
//
// **Whether the buffer was ever there is not knowable from here, and that is the point.** The image may well
// have shipped one when 0.85.0 measured a fifth off the sweep, and lost it in a later image bump — a silent
// regression nothing would report, because the `ini_set()` keeps succeeding either way. Pinning the buffer
// explicitly is what stops that happening again, which is why it is now stated in two places instead of
// assumed in none.
//
// The buffer is now set at startup, where it has to be: `.ddev/php/opcache-jit.ini` locally and `ini-values`
// on setup-php in `.github/workflows/tests.yml`. **Both of those must stay in step with each other**, because
// a local timing that does not match CI's is worse than no local timing at all.
//
// This call is kept rather than deleted, because it is still the thing that turns the JIT on in an environment
// that has a buffer but no mode — a bare `php` on a host, say. Silenced because a build without opcache should
// run slowly rather than not at all.
//
// **This is where the JIT actually pays, and it is measured: 1.57x.** A bare `scene:stack` sweep in one process
// is 18.1 s with it and 28.5 s without, three repeats each. An evenly spread 40-scene compile loop is
// 0.1449 s/scene against 0.2327 s/scene. The PHPUnit suite, by contrast, is 22:06.248 against 22:06.927 — the
// same number twice — so the benefit belongs to the commands rather than to the tests, and TOOL-22 holds the
// open question of why. The old comment's "2m31s against 2m00s" is not carried forward, because there is no
// way to tell now whether that run had a buffer at all.
@ini_set('opcache.jit', 'tracing');

require dirname(__DIR__).'/vendor/autoload.php';

use App\Command\BuildAllCommand;
use App\Command\CatalogCommand;
use App\Command\LibraryBuildCommand;
use App\Command\LoadPlanCommand;
use App\Command\ModelsBuildCommand;
use App\Command\SceneBuildCommand;
use App\Command\SceneFlyThroughCommand;
use App\Command\ScenePackCommand;
use App\Command\SceneRenderCommand;
use App\Command\SceneStackCommand;
use App\Command\SpecsValidateCommand;
use Symfony\Component\Console\Application;

// **The version is read from `composer.json` rather than written here.** It had been a literal since 0.65.0
// and was 31 releases stale by the time anybody looked, which is what a number duplicated between two files
// does. `composer.json` is the one this project's own convention bumps, so it is the one that decides.
$version = 'unknown';
$manifest = json_decode((string) @file_get_contents(dirname(__DIR__).'/composer.json'), true);
if (is_array($manifest) && is_string($manifest['version'] ?? null)) {
    $version = $manifest['version'];
}

$app = new Application('sdwa5-3d', $version);
$app->addCommands([
    new SpecsValidateCommand(),
    new ModelsBuildCommand(),
    new LibraryBuildCommand(),
    new SceneStackCommand(),
    new SceneBuildCommand(),
    new SceneFlyThroughCommand(),
    new ScenePackCommand(),
    new SceneRenderCommand(),
    new CatalogCommand(),
    new LoadPlanCommand(),
    new BuildAllCommand(),
]);
$app->run();
